From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alex Sherwin <alex-sherwin@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 2 Jul 2026 12:43:03 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] terminal: bulk lane for APC string payloads + kitty frame
 presize (purrtty patch)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The VT stream's SIMD fast path only covers the ground state; inside an APC
string (kitty graphics payloads) every byte walked a five-layer per-byte
pipeline — parse-table lookup, apc_put action dispatch, handler vt() call,
apc.Handler.feed, kitty CommandParser.feed, one-byte ArrayList append. A
kitty video frame is megabytes of opaque base64 through exactly that state,
which made VTWrite the dominant per-frame cost of the gatOS screen stream.

consumeUntilGround now has a bulk lane: in sos_pm_apc_string, every byte
below 0x80 other than CAN/SUB/ESC is a bare apc_put with no state change
(parse_table.zig), so whole runs are scanned out and handed to the handler
in one call. Handlers opt in by declaring vtApcPutMany (semantically
identical to one vt(.apc_put, byte) per byte; non-opting handlers keep the
exact per-byte path, including prospective vtRaw interceptors). The terminal
handler routes it through new slice variants — apc.Handler.feedSlice and
kitty Parser.feedSlice — whose .data state appends the remaining run in one
memcpy; identification and control-key bytes still step per byte.

LoadingImage.init also presizes the payload accumulator for uncompressed
direct transmissions (the first chunk fixes s*v*bpp), removing the ~1.5x
growth-realloc memmoves (~10-15 MB of pure copy per 5 MB frame).

Tests: kitty Parser.feedSlice equivalence at every split point + payload cap
parity; apc.Handler.feedSlice equivalence across the identify/kitty states;
a stream-level differential test pinning bulk-lane bytes/starts/ends equal
to the per-byte handler (and that the lane engages). Full test-lib-vt green.

